# Giocci Live: Hands-on for Offloading Computation Across the Edge, in Real Time

## Introduction

Welcome to [Code BEAM Europe 2026 Informal Space](https://codebeameurope.com/informal-space/)!

This Livebook contains the hands-on material for the following session.

* **Giocci Live: Offloading Computation Across the Edge, in Real Time**
  * Day 2 Thursday 22 October 10:50–11:30
  * Yutaka Kikuchi & Hideki Takase

### What is Giocci?

[Giocci](https://github.com/biyooon-ex/giocci_platform) is a resource-permeating computing platform for wide-area distributed systems.
We introduced the concepts and architecture of Giocci in detail at [Goatmire Elixir 2025](https://2025.goatmire.com/)!

* Slides: https://speakerdeck.com/takasehideki/giocci-a-resource-permeating-computing-platform-for-wide-area-distributed-systems
* Video: https://goatmire.bold.video/v/9wjke

Here are the features of Giocci in three lines.

1. Giocci has a three-layer architecture: Client, Relay, and Engine.
2. You can offload heavy Elixir processing from low-power devices to powerful devices or servers.
3. For communication between resources, we use [zenohex](https://github.com/biyooon-ex/zenohex), an Elixir client library for the lightweight and flexible [Zenoh](https://zenoh.io/) protocol (this is also one of our products!).

We have introduced Giocci at Code BEAM and other conferences several times. But the quickest way to understand this platform is to try it yourself. So we prepared this step-by-step hands-on session. Please give it a try and have fun!

### Let's get started

This Livebook is available here.

* https://github.com/biyooon-ex/nerves_livebook/blob/v0.19.0-gioccidemo/priv/giocci/giocci_live_handson.livemd

It also works with the regular [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/) native app on your PC.

At the session, we also have Raspberry Pi 5 devices with Nerves Livebook installed.
You need to connect to the Wi-Fi AP for this session and open the Nerves device in your web browser. We will share these details at the venue.

Please try it on your own device too!

## 1. Environment preparation (Livebook Setup cell)

To use Giocci, you need computers running Zenohd, Relay, and Engine.
See the following guides to install them.

* Zenohd: See the "Installing the Zenoh router" section on [this page](https://zenoh.io/docs/getting-started/installation/).
* Giocci Relay: https://github.com/biyooon-ex/giocci_platform/blob/v0.6.3/apps/giocci_relay/README.md
* Giocci Engine: https://github.com/biyooon-ex/giocci_platform/blob/v0.6.3/apps/giocci_engine/README.md

To set up your own environment, prepare a computer with port 7447 open and docker available, then follow the guides above. You mainly need to edit `config/giocci_{relay,engine}.exs` and `config/zenoh.json5`. You can also run all three on your own PC.

For the session, we have prepared the following environment on Azure.

| server                | spec             | region               |
| --------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------- |
| biyooon-zenohd        | 2 vcpu / 8 GiB   | Belgium Central      |
| biyooon-relay         | 2 vcpu / 8 GiB   | Belgium Central      |
| biyooon-engine-yowai  | 2 vcpu / 8 GiB   | Belgium Central      |
| biyooon-engine-futsuu | 4 vcpu / 16 GiB  | Germany West Central |
| biyooon-engine-tsuyoi | 16 vcpu / 64 GiB | Belgium Central      |

In regular Livebook, the next cell installs Kino, Giocci, Req, and StbImage.
On Nerves, we assume that Giocci, Zenohex, Kino, Req, and StbImage are included in the firmware. The cell checks the dependencies in the firmware.

```elixir
if Code.ensure_loaded?(Nerves.Runtime) do
  for module <- [Kino, Giocci, Giocci.DynamicModule, Giocci.Inspect, Req, StbImage] do
    unless Code.ensure_loaded?(module), do: raise("The firmware needs #{inspect(module)}")
  end
else
  Mix.install([
    {:kino, "== 0.19.1"},
    {:giocci, "== 0.6.3", runtime: false},
    {:req, "~> 0.5.8"},
    {:stb_image, "~> 0.6.10"}
  ])
end
```

## 2. Get, prepare, and display the input image

This hands-on session uses image processing as an example.

First, use Req to get the input image from a URL and resize it to a width of 320 px while keeping its aspect ratio.
To use a different image, change `image_url`.

We also define the helper functions used in the following steps.

```elixir
# Fetch the image from the Elixir runtime and pass the HTTP body directly to the image decoder.
image_url =
  "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2108086088661450752/G4pqmMEl_400x400.jpg"

response = Req.get!(image_url, decode_body: false, retry: false)
unless response.status == 200,
  do: raise("Failed to fetch the image (HTTP #{response.status})")

input_image = StbImage.read_binary!(response.body, channels: 3)

# Calculate the height from the aspect ratio. Do not crop to a square or add padding.
{source_height, source_width, 3} = input_image.shape
width = 320
height = max(1, round(source_height * width / source_width))
rgb = StbImage.resize(input_image, height, width).data

defmodule GiocciHandson.Notebook do
  def now, do: System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
  def ms(us), do: Float.round(us / 1000, 3)

  def image(pixels, width, height, channels) do
    Kino.Image.new(<<height::32, width::32, channels::8, pixels::binary>>, :pixel)
  end

  def panel(title, pixels, width, height, channels) do
    Kino.Layout.grid([Kino.Markdown.new("**#{title}**"), image(pixels, width, height, channels)],
      gap: 8
    )
  end

  def check!(result, width, height, channels, tag) do
    case result do
      %{
        pixels: pixels,
        compute_us: us,
        tag: ^tag,
        width: ^width,
        height: ^height,
        channels: ^channels
      }
      when is_binary(pixels) and byte_size(pixels) == width * height * channels and
             is_integer(us) and us >= 0 ->
        result

      other ->
        raise "Invalid image processing result: #{inspect(other, limit: 5)}"
    end
  end

  def comparison(rgb, width, height, local, local_us, remote, remote_us, label) do
    same = if local.pixels == remote.pixels, do: "Identical", else: "Different (check rounding errors or the distributed version)"

    Kino.Layout.grid([
      Kino.Markdown.new("""
      **#{label}** — Byte-by-byte output comparison: **#{same}**

      | Execution destination | Total call time (ms) | Kernel time (ms) |
      | --- | ---: | ---: |
      | Local | #{ms(local_us)} | #{ms(local.compute_us)} |
      | Giocci | #{ms(remote_us)} | #{ms(remote.compute_us)} |
      """),
      Kino.Layout.grid(
        [
          panel("Input", rgb, width, height, 3),
          panel("Local", local.pixels, width, height, local.channels),
          panel("Giocci", remote.pixels, width, height, remote.channels)
        ],
        columns: 3
      )
    ])
  end
end

Kino.render(GiocciHandson.Notebook.panel("Input image / #{width}×#{height}", rgb, width, height, 3))

:ok
```

## 3. Grayscale: run locally first

Convert RGB to grayscale using the integer weighted sum `(77*r + 150*g + 29*b) / 256`.
`run/4` returns the image, the computation time, and a tag used to match the result to the call.
Define the Grayscale module and run it locally first.

When defining the module, add `use Giocci.DynamicModule` so that Giocci keeps the compiled BEAM.

```elixir
defmodule GiocciHandson.Grayscale do
  use Giocci.DynamicModule

  def process(rgb, width, height) do
    unless width > 0 and height > 0 and byte_size(rgb) == width * height * 3,
      do: raise(ArgumentError, "invalid RGB image")

    # Approximate the weighted RGB sum with integers. The result has 1 byte per pixel.
    for <<r, g, b <- rgb>>, into: <<>> do
      <<div(77 * r + 150 * g + 29 * b, 256)>>
    end
  end

  def run(rgb, width, height, tag) do
    {us, pixels} = :timer.tc(fn -> process(rgb, width, height) end)
    %{pixels: pixels, width: width, height: height, channels: 1, compute_us: us, tag: tag}
  end
end

{grayscale_local_us, grayscale_local} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> GiocciHandson.Grayscale.run(rgb, width, height, :still) end)

IO.puts(
  "Local / Grayscale: Total call time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(grayscale_local_us)} ms / Kernel time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(grayscale_local.compute_us)} ms"
)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.image(grayscale_local.pixels, width, height, grayscale_local.channels)
)

:ok
```

## 4. Run Grayscale with Giocci

Now, let's try Giocci!

There are three steps.

### a) Register the Client with the Relay

Use [`register_client/2`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/Giocci.html#register_client/2) to register the Client with the Relay.
You only need to do this once when the Client starts.

Set the following four values.

* Client name: You do not need to change this for this hands-on session.
* Relay name to register with:
  * If you use a Raspberry Pi 5 at the venue, use the value we provide.
  * If you have your own server, use its Relay name.
* Server running Zenohd:
  * If you use a Raspberry Pi 5 at the venue, use the value we provide.
  * If you have your own server, use its IP address.
  * If you run it on your own PC, you do not need to change `127.0.0.1`.
* Zenoh key prefix (optional): You usually do not need to change this.

In a regular mix project, you can set these values in [`config/config.exs`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/readme.html#configuration) and `zenoh.json5`.

After setting these values, run the next cell.

```elixir
client_name = "image-demo-#{System.pid()}-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"
relay = "giocci_relay"
endpoints = ["tcp/127.0.0.1:7447"]
key_prefix = ""

Application.put_env(:giocci, :client_name, client_name)
System.put_env("ZENOHD_CONNECT_ENDPOINTS", Enum.join(endpoints, ","))
Application.put_env(:giocci, :zenoh_config_file_path, nil)
Application.put_env(:giocci, :key_prefix, key_prefix)

if List.keymember?(Application.started_applications(), :giocci, 0),
  do: Application.stop(:giocci)
{:ok, _} = Application.ensure_all_started(:giocci)

:ok = Giocci.register_client(relay)

IO.puts("Registered Client #{client_name} with #{relay}")
```

### b) Save the module

Use [`save_module/3`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/Giocci.html#save_module/3) to save the Client's module.
You only need to do this once for each module. If you change the module definition, run it again.

Pass the Relay name and the module name as arguments.

```elixir
:ok = Giocci.save_module(relay, GiocciHandson.Grayscale)
```

### c) Execute the function

Use [`exec_func/3`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/Giocci.html#exec_func/3) to execute a function on an Engine.
Pass the Relay name and `{module_name, function_name, arguments}` as arguments.

With this API, the Client asks the Relay for a suitable Engine for remote execution (the current implementation uses round-robin selection...).

```elixir
{grayscale_remote_us, grayscale_remote} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Giocci.exec_func(relay, {GiocciHandson.Grayscale, :run, [rgb, width, height, :still]})
  end)

GiocciHandson.Notebook.check!(grayscale_remote, width, height, 1, :still)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.comparison(
    rgb,
    width,
    height,
    grayscale_local,
    grayscale_local_us,
    grayscale_remote,
    grayscale_remote_us,
    "Grayscale"
  )
)

:ok
```

## 5. Edge detection

Hmm, Grayscale may not get much faster... It is probably because this is a light task even for the Client.
Let's run a slightly heavier task remotely with Giocci.

The Sobel filter in the next demo detects edges in the image (the module converts the image to grayscale first).
The intensity is `abs(gx) + abs(gy)`, capped at 255. The outermost 1-pixel border is black.

Define the edge detection (Sobel) module and run it locally first.
Use `use Giocci.DynamicModule` so that Giocci keeps the compiled BEAM.

```elixir
defmodule GiocciHandson.Sobel do
  use Giocci.DynamicModule

  def process(rgb, width, height) do
    unless width > 0 and height > 0 and byte_size(rgb) == width * height * 3,
      do: raise(ArgumentError, "invalid RGB image")

    gray = grayscale(rgb)

    for y <- 0..(height - 1), x <- 0..(width - 1), into: <<>> do
      if x == 0 or y == 0 or x == width - 1 or y == height - 1 do
        <<0>>
      else
        p = y * width + x
        a = :binary.at(gray, p - width - 1)
        b = :binary.at(gray, p - width)
        c = :binary.at(gray, p - width + 1)
        d = :binary.at(gray, p - 1)
        f = :binary.at(gray, p + 1)
        g = :binary.at(gray, p + width - 1)
        h = :binary.at(gray, p + width)
        i = :binary.at(gray, p + width + 1)
        gx = -a + c - 2 * d + 2 * f - g + i
        gy = -a - 2 * b - c + g + 2 * h + i
        # Use an L1 approximation for gradient intensity. The border is black, and intensity is capped at 255.
        <<min(255, abs(gx) + abs(gy))>>
      end
    end
  end

  # Keep this function inside the module so that save_module only needs to send this module.
  defp grayscale(rgb) do
    for <<r, g, b <- rgb>>, into: <<>>, do: <<div(77 * r + 150 * g + 29 * b, 256)>>
  end

  def run(rgb, width, height, tag) do
    # Include grayscale conversion in the Sobel processing time.
    {us, pixels} = :timer.tc(fn -> process(rgb, width, height) end)
    %{pixels: pixels, width: width, height: height, channels: 1, compute_us: us, tag: tag}
  end
end

{sobel_local_us, sobel_local} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> GiocciHandson.Sobel.run(rgb, width, height, :still) end)

IO.puts(
  "Local / Edge detection (Sobel): Total call time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(sobel_local_us)} ms / Kernel time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(sobel_local.compute_us)} ms"
)

Kino.render(GiocciHandson.Notebook.image(sobel_local.pixels, width, height, sobel_local.channels))

:ok
```

Distribute this edge detection (Sobel) module to the server too, and process the same RGB image with Giocci.
Display the total call time and kernel time, and compare the output with Local.

```elixir
# Distribute the dynamically defined module using only its module name.
:ok = Giocci.save_module(relay, GiocciHandson.Sobel)

{sobel_remote_us, sobel_remote} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Giocci.exec_func(relay, {GiocciHandson.Sobel, :run, [rgb, width, height, :still]})
  end)

GiocciHandson.Notebook.check!(sobel_remote, width, height, 1, :still)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.comparison(
    rgb,
    width,
    height,
    sobel_local,
    sobel_local_us,
    sobel_remote,
    sobel_remote_us,
    "Edge detection (Sobel)"
  )
)

:ok
```

## 6. Gaussian blur

Let's run an even heavier task!

Apply a Gaussian filter to a color image.
Split the convolution into two passes, horizontal and vertical. Use the edge pixels for positions outside the image.
Also, split each pass into groups of rows and process them in parallel based on `System.schedulers_online()` on the execution device.

Define the Gaussian blur module and run it locally first. Use `use Giocci.DynamicModule` so that Giocci keeps the compiled BEAM.

```elixir
defmodule GiocciHandson.Gaussian do
  use Giocci.DynamicModule

  @radius 8
  @sigma 4.0
  @weights for d <- -@radius..@radius, do: {d, :math.exp(-d * d / (2 * @sigma * @sigma))}
  @weight_sum Enum.reduce(@weights, 0.0, fn {_, v}, sum -> sum + v end)
  @kernel Enum.map(@weights, fn {d, v} -> {d, v / @weight_sum} end)

  def process(rgb, width, height) do
    unless width > 0 and height > 0 and byte_size(rgb) == width * height * 3,
      do: raise(ArgumentError, "invalid RGB image")

    # Use the scheduler count on the execution device. Do not create more Tasks than rows.
    concurrency = min(System.schedulers_online(), height)

    # Split the 17×17 2D convolution into horizontal and vertical passes. Blur each RGB channel separately.
    # Store intermediate results in a float64 binary to avoid rounding in the horizontal pass.
    horizontal =
      parallel_rows(height, concurrency, fn rows ->
        for y <- rows, x <- 0..(width - 1), channel <- 0..2, into: <<>> do
          value =
            Enum.reduce(@kernel, 0.0, fn {dx, weight}, sum ->
              p = (y * width + clamp(x + dx, width - 1)) * 3 + channel
              sum + weight * :binary.at(rgb, p)
            end)

          <<value::float-little-64>>
        end
      end)

    parallel_rows(height, concurrency, fn rows ->
      for y <- rows, x <- 0..(width - 1), channel <- 0..2, into: <<>> do
        value =
          Enum.reduce(@kernel, 0.0, fn {dy, weight}, sum ->
            p = (clamp(y + dy, height - 1) * width + x) * 3 + channel
            <<value::float-little-64>> = binary_part(horizontal, p * 8, 8)
            sum + weight * value
          end)

        <<clamp(round(value), 255)>>
      end
    end)
  end

  # Use edge pixels for positions outside the image (edge clamp).
  defp clamp(value, maximum), do: max(0, min(maximum, value))

  # With one scheduler, as on Pi Zero, process sequentially without creating Tasks.
  defp parallel_rows(height, 1, fun), do: fun.(0..(height - 1))

  # Process groups of rows and join the results in row order.
  # Avoid the default 5-second Task timeout on slow devices. This is separate from the Giocci timeout.
  defp parallel_rows(height, concurrency, fun) do
    chunk = div(height + concurrency - 1, concurrency)

    0..(height - 1)
    |> Enum.chunk_every(chunk)
    |> Task.async_stream(fun, max_concurrency: concurrency, ordered: true, timeout: :infinity)
    |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, binary} -> binary end)
    |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  end

  def run(rgb, width, height, tag) do
    {us, pixels} = :timer.tc(fn -> process(rgb, width, height) end)
    %{pixels: pixels, width: width, height: height, channels: 3, compute_us: us, tag: tag}
  end
end

{gaussian_local_us, gaussian_local} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> GiocciHandson.Gaussian.run(rgb, width, height, :still) end)

IO.puts(
  "Local / Gaussian blur: Total call time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(gaussian_local_us)} ms / Kernel time #{GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(gaussian_local.compute_us)} ms"
)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.image(gaussian_local.pixels, width, height, gaussian_local.channels)
)

:ok
```

Distribute this module to the server too, and process the same RGB image with Giocci. Display the total call time and kernel time, and compare the output with Local.

```elixir
# Distribute the dynamically defined module using only its module name.
:ok = Giocci.save_module(relay, GiocciHandson.Gaussian)

{gaussian_remote_us, gaussian_remote} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Giocci.exec_func(relay, {GiocciHandson.Gaussian, :run, [rgb, width, height, :still]})
  end)

GiocciHandson.Notebook.check!(gaussian_remote, width, height, 3, :still)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.comparison(
    rgb,
    width,
    height,
    gaussian_local,
    gaussian_local_us,
    gaussian_remote,
    gaussian_remote_us,
    "Gaussian blur"
  )
)

:ok
```

### Run with the asynchronous API

Giocci can also execute functions asynchronously.
After sending the execution request, you can continue local work without waiting for the result.

[`exec_func_async/4`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/Giocci.html#exec_func_async/4) returns `:ok` when the send request is accepted. The computation result arrives at the specified process as `{:giocci, result}`.

The module has already been distributed above, so we can use it as it is.

First, define a small receiver process that keeps the result across cells.

```elixir
defmodule GiocciHandson.AsyncResult do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(_), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, nil)

  @impl true
  def init(_), do: {:ok, %{result: nil, waiter: nil}}

  @impl true
  def handle_info({:giocci, result}, state) do
    received = {GiocciHandson.Notebook.now(), result}
    if state.waiter, do: GenServer.reply(state.waiter, received)
    {:noreply, %{state | result: received, waiter: nil}}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:result, from, %{result: nil} = state) do
    # If the result is not ready, reply when it arrives.
    {:noreply, %{state | waiter: from}}
  end

  def handle_call(:result, _from, state), do: {:reply, state.result, state}
end
```

Start the receiver process and send the Gaussian task asynchronously. This cell does not wait for image processing to finish.
Let Kino manage the receiver process.

```elixir
{:ok, gaussian_receiver} = Kino.start_child({GiocciHandson.AsyncResult, []})
gaussian_async_tag = make_ref()

# Measure from just before the call until the result arrives at the dedicated process.
gaussian_async_started = GiocciHandson.Notebook.now()
:ok = Giocci.exec_func_async(
  relay,
  {GiocciHandson.Gaussian, :run, [rgb, width, height, gaussian_async_tag]},
  gaussian_receiver
)

IO.puts("Request sent. You can do other local work while waiting for the result.")
:ok
```

Get the received result and compare it with the Local image. If it has already arrived, display it immediately. Otherwise, wait for up to 5 seconds.

```elixir
{gaussian_async_received, gaussian_async_result} = GenServer.call(gaussian_receiver, :result)
gaussian_async_us = gaussian_async_received - gaussian_async_started

GiocciHandson.Notebook.check!(gaussian_async_result, width, height, 3, gaussian_async_tag)

Kino.render(
  GiocciHandson.Notebook.comparison(
    rgb, width, height,
    gaussian_local, gaussian_local_us,
    gaussian_async_result, gaussian_async_us,
    "Gaussian blur / Giocci Async"
  )
)

:ok
```

## 7. Check connections and saved code with Inspect

[`Giocci.Inspect`](https://giocci.hexdocs.pm/Giocci.Inspect.html) lets you check Giocci connections, registrations, and BEAMs saved on the Relay (it was added in [v0.6.2](https://github.com/biyooon-ex/giocci_platform/releases/tag/v0.6.2), released just last week!).
After running the three apps, review the remote execution environments and the saved code.

First, check your Client name, key_prefix, Zenoh ID, the IDs of connected Routers/Peers, and registered Relays.

`list_relays` uses this Client's registration history to ask each Relay about its current registration status.
If only some Relays can be confirmed, it returns `{:warn, confirmed_relays, issues}`, so we display the return value as it is.

`connection_info` provides information about your Zenoh session.

```elixir
IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.connection_info(), label: "Client / Zenoh connection info")
IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.list_relays(), label: "Registered Relay check")

:ok
```

Next, check the Relay's execution environment and response. The `rtt_ms` value from `ping_relay` is the round-trip time of a Giocci Query/Reply.

```elixir
IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.relay_info(relay), label: "Relay environment")
IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.ping_relay(relay), label: "Relay response check")
```

Get the Engines registered with the Relay, and check each Engine's execution environment and response.
`engine_info` and `ping_engine` query the Engine directly using only its name.

`schedulers_online` is the number of BEAM schedulers running in parallel.

```elixir
{:ok, engines} = Giocci.Inspect.list_engines(relay)
IO.inspect(engines, label: "Registered Engines")

for engine <- engines do
  IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.engine_info(engine), label: "Engine #{engine} environment")
  IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.ping_engine(engine), label: "Engine #{engine} response check")
end

:ok
```

Finally, check the modules saved on the Relay under this Client name, their public functions, and their saved information.
If you have followed the steps in order, the three modules for Grayscale, Sobel, and Gaussian are already saved.
`list_modules` returns module names as strings. You can pass them directly to the following APIs.
`list_functions` returns function names and argument counts, such as `{"process", 3}` and `{"run", 4}`. It does not include private functions.

```elixir
{:ok, saved_modules} = Giocci.Inspect.list_modules(relay)
IO.inspect(saved_modules, label: "Saved modules for this Client")

for module <- saved_modules do
  IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.list_functions(relay, module), label: "#{module} public functions")
  IO.inspect(Giocci.Inspect.module_info(relay, module), label: "#{module} saved info")
end

:ok
```

## 8. Run each of the three apps three times and compare

Can Giocci actually make processing faster?

For each app, alternate between Local and the Giocci synchronous API, running each three times.
We measure the total function call time, including communication, and the kernel time for the filter itself.
When multiple Engines are registered, the execution destination changes between measurements.

The Giocci total time includes Engine selection, sending and receiving data, waiting, and receiving the result.
For light tasks such as Grayscale, we can also observe that Giocci may be slower because of communication time.
Measure three times with the same input image and display each value and the average. Also display the Local/Giocci images from the third run of each app side by side.

```elixir
apps = [
  {"Grayscale", GiocciHandson.Grayscale, 1},
  {"Edge detection", GiocciHandson.Sobel, 1},
  {"Gaussian blur", GiocciHandson.Gaussian, 3}
]

benchmark_results =
  for {label, module, channels} <- apps do
    runs =
      for trial <- 1..3 do
        tag = {:benchmark, trial}

        {local_us, local_result} =
          :timer.tc(fn -> apply(module, :run, [rgb, width, height, tag]) end)

        {remote_us, remote_result} =
          :timer.tc(fn ->
            Giocci.exec_func(relay, {module, :run, [rgb, width, height, tag]})
          end)

        GiocciHandson.Notebook.check!(remote_result, width, height, channels, tag)

        %{local_us: local_us, remote_us: remote_us, local: local_result, remote: remote_result}
      end

    {label, runs}
  end

rows =
  for {label, runs} <- benchmark_results, side <- [:local, :remote] do
    key = if side == :local, do: :local_us, else: :remote_us
    times = Enum.map(runs, &Map.fetch!(&1, key))
    mean = Enum.sum(times) / 3
    compute_mean = Enum.sum(Enum.map(runs, &Map.fetch!(&1, side).compute_us)) / 3

    values =
      Enum.map_join(
        times ++ [mean, compute_mean],
        " | ",
        &to_string(GiocciHandson.Notebook.ms(&1))
      )

    "| #{label} | #{side} | #{values} |"
  end

Kino.render(
  Kino.Markdown.new("""
  | App | Execution destination | Call 1 (ms) | Call 2 (ms) | Call 3 (ms) | Average call time (ms) | Average kernel time (ms) |
  | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
  #{Enum.join(rows, "\n")}
  """)
)

comparisons =
  for {label, runs} <- benchmark_results do
    last = List.last(runs)

    GiocciHandson.Notebook.comparison(
      rgb,
      width,
      height,
      last.local,
      last.local_us,
      last.remote,
      last.remote_us,
      "#{label} / Run 3"
    )
  end

Kino.render(Kino.Layout.grid(comparisons, gap: 24))

:ok
```

## Did it work?

